        <div id="why-gravity-cant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a Gravity Pitcher Cannot Do Reverse Osmosis
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search for a reverse osmosis water filter pitcher and you will find two kinds of product. One is an ordinary gravity pitcher with RO in the marketing copy. The other is a plug-in countertop machine that pumps water through a real RO membrane and delivers it into a carafe or pitcher. Only the second is reverse osmosis. The reason is physics, not branding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reverse osmosis works by pushing water through a membrane with pores small enough to reject dissolved salts, metals and most other dissolved contaminants. Water naturally wants to move the other way, from the clean side to the salty side. That is ordinary osmosis. To reverse it, you have to apply more pressure than the osmotic pressure of your water, plus enough extra to get a useful flow. A common rule of thumb is about 1 psi of osmotic pressure for every 100 mg/L of dissolved solids, so typical tap water at 300 to 500 mg/L needs several psi just to break even.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pitcher cannot supply that. The pressure at the bottom of a column of water is about 0.036 psi per inch of height. A tall pitcher reservoir holding 10 inches of water pushes down at roughly a third of a psi. That is enough to trickle water through carbon granules, which is all a gravity pitcher filter does. It is nowhere near enough to drive water through an RO membrane, which is why under-sink RO systems run on household line pressure, typically 40 to 80 psi, and often add a booster pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when a gravity pitcher says it removes dissolved solids, read the fine print. Some use ion-exchange resin to cut TDS, which is a different technology with a much shorter life. That approach can lower the TDS reading on a meter, but it is not reverse osmosis and it does not carry an NSF/ANSI 58 certification, which is the RO standard.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If it does not plug in, it is not reverse osmosis.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every true RO pitcher on the market is a countertop appliance with a pump. The pitcher or carafe is where the purified water ends up, not where the filtering happens.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What an RO Pitcher Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            A countertop RO unit is a small appliance about the size of a bread maker or a large coffee machine. You fill a tap water tank on the back or top by hand, press a button, and an electric pump pushes that water through a sediment pre-filter, a carbon stage, the RO membrane and usually a carbon polishing stage. Purified water ends up in a clean water tank or a removable carafe. The water the membrane rejects goes into a separate waste compartment or back into the tap tank, depending on the design.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last point is the difference between models. AquaTru's units send concentrated water back to the tap tank, which you empty and refill. Waterdrop's M6SL keeps waste in its own 1.5 liter compartment. Either way, you are the drain. There is no plumbing, no hole in the counter and no landlord to ask, which is the whole appeal for renters and for kitchens where the under-sink cabinet is already full.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pitcher part is where these units differ most for the person using them. The AquaTru Classic dispenses from a clean water tank with a spout, like a water cooler. The AquaTru Carafe, Waterdrop M6SL, Waterdrop C2SL and Bluevua ROPOT all deliver into a removable glass vessel you can carry to the table or put in the fridge. If what you wanted from a pitcher was cold filtered water on a fridge shelf, those glass-carafe models are the closest match.
          </p>

          <p>
            They also have limits a pitcher does not. They need an outlet. They take up more counter space than a coffee maker. They are slower than pouring through carbon, because a membrane is slow. And they cost several times what a gravity pitcher costs. In exchange, they do what no gravity pitcher can: remove most dissolved solids, fluoride, nitrate and arsenic, with published certification on the better models.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are deciding between this and a standard pitcher, our main <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitcher guide</a> covers what carbon pitchers do well, and for many city water supplies a certified carbon pitcher is all you need. RO is for water problems carbon cannot touch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
