        <div id="tds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. TDS and the Number on the Display
          </h2>

          <p>
            TDS, total dissolved solids, is the measure most countertop RO units put on the front panel, and it is the measure most buyers check with a cheap pen meter. It is a useful number with a narrow meaning. TDS is the total mass of dissolved material in the water, mostly minerals such as calcium, magnesium, sodium, bicarbonate and chloride. It does not distinguish between harmless minerals and harmful contaminants.
          </p>

          <p>
            That means a high TDS reading is not automatically unsafe, and a low one is not automatically safe. Lead at a dangerous level barely moves a TDS meter, because the amount involved is tiny. What TDS does well is tell you whether the membrane is working. A good RO membrane should cut TDS by a large fraction. AquaTru's data sheet lists an 87.1% average TDS reduction on the NSF/ANSI 58 test, against a minimum requirement of 75%.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the TDS display on the Bluevua and Waterdrop units is more useful than it sounds. When the reading after a cycle starts creeping up week by week, the membrane is wearing out or scaling. It is an early warning that a gallon count alone cannot give you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be careful with units that add minerals back after the membrane. A remineralization stage raises TDS on purpose, so the reading after that stage will be higher than the membrane alone produces. Compare readings from the same unit over time, not against another brand's advertised number.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
