        <div id="waste-ratio" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Waste Ratio, Explained Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every RO system rejects some water. The membrane splits incoming water into purified water and a concentrated stream carrying what it rejected. Countertop makers advertise this as a pure-to-drain ratio, and the better units here run around 3:1, meaning three parts drinking water to one part waste. Waterdrop lists 3:1 for both the M6SL and C2SL, and the Bluevua listing also gives 3:1.
          </p>

          <p>
            AquaTru uses the NSF/ANSI 58 terms instead. Its data sheet gives an efficiency rating of 75% and a recovery rating of 80%. Efficiency is the share of all incoming water that becomes drinking water under typical use, so 75% means one gallon of waste for every three gallons of purified water, the same as 3:1. Recovery is measured on the membrane alone with no storage tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a countertop unit, waste is also a chore, not just a number. Every gallon of purified water at 3:1 produces about a third of a gallon of concentrate, which you pour out by hand. It is fine for watering plants that tolerate some salt, rinsing dishes or mopping. Do not use it for houseplants that are sensitive to minerals, and do not drink it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older under-sink RO systems could waste four gallons or more for every gallon they made, which is where RO's wasteful reputation comes from. A countertop unit at 3:1 is far better than that. If waste matters to you, choose by the published ratio or efficiency figure and ignore models that do not state one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Water In vs Water Out at 3:1</p>
