        <div id="remineralisation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Remineralisation and Alkaline Stages
          </h2>

          <p>
            RO water tastes flat to many people, because the membrane removes the calcium, magnesium and bicarbonate that give tap water its character. RO water also tends to read slightly acidic, because carbon dioxide passes through the membrane while the buffering minerals do not. Remineralization stages add a small amount of minerals back to change both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Waterdrop's M6SL and C2SL include a mineral stage, and Waterdrop says the C2SL adds potassium, calcium, sodium and magnesium. Bluevua's ROPOT-Lite(UV) ends with a mineral filter. AquaTru sells a VOC filter with a pH+ mineral boost as an option, which its data sheet lists as part AT2004 on the same 12 month or 600 gallon schedule as the standard VOC filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be realistic about what these stages do. The mineral amounts they add are small next to what you get from food, so they are a taste adjustment, not a supplement. Health claims for alkaline water go well beyond the evidence. Waterdrop itself notes that its mineral benefit claims come from internal evaluation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical question is whether you like the taste. Some people prefer pure RO water and find remineralized water slightly chalky. Others find straight RO flat and want the mineral stage. If you are not sure, pick a unit where the mineral stage is optional, such as the AquaTru with its pH+ filter, rather than one where it is built into the only filter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Certifications: NSF/ANSI 58 vs 372 vs Marketing
          </h2>

          <p>
            The certification that matters for any reverse osmosis claim is NSF/ANSI 58, the drinking water RO standard. It certifies TDS reduction and lets the maker list specific contaminants such as lead, fluoride, arsenic, nitrate and chromium. A countertop unit certified to 58 by IAPMO, NSF or WQA has had its membrane performance checked against a public protocol.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other standards add coverage on top. NSF/ANSI 42 covers chlorine and taste, 53 covers health contaminants such as VOCs and PFOA/PFOS, and 401 covers some pharmaceuticals and microplastics. AquaTru's data sheet lists all of them for the Classic family, with contaminant-by-contaminant reduction figures, which is why it is our top pick despite its plastic tanks.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI 372 is the one to watch. It certifies that a product's materials meet a low-lead content limit. It is a materials standard, not a performance standard, and it says nothing about what the filter removes. A countertop RO unit that is certified only to 372 can truthfully say it is NSF certified while having no certified contaminant reduction at all. Bluevua's displayed NSF certificate references 372, and Waterdrop's C2SL shows no certification on Waterdrop's own comparison table.
          </p>

