        <div id="countertop-vs-undersink" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Countertop RO vs Under-Sink RO vs a Pitcher
          </h2>

          <p>
            Countertop RO sits between two other options, and choosing it should mean you have ruled both out. A gravity carbon pitcher is far cheaper and simpler and handles chlorine, taste and, if certified, lead. Under-sink RO does everything a countertop unit does, makes more water, and runs to its own faucet without a tank to fill by hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose countertop RO if you rent, if your cabinet has no room, if you do not want to drill a counter for a second faucet, or if you want to take the unit with you when you move. Choose under-sink if you own the house, use more than a gallon or two of drinking water a day, and want RO at the tap without thinking about it. Our <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">under sink water filter guide</a> covers the plumbed RO systems, including tankless models.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a gravity pitcher if your water report does not show TDS, fluoride, nitrate or arsenic problems. For chlorine taste and a documented lead claim, a certified carbon pitcher at a fraction of the price is the honest recommendation. If you want that pitcher in glass, our <a href="/best-glass-water-filter-pitcher/">glass water filter pitcher guide</a> covers the models that publish real certifications.
          </p>

