        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Filter Costs and the Two-Year Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Countertop RO is expensive to buy and moderate to run. The membrane is the long-life part, typically one to two years. The carbon and sediment stages wear faster. Comparing units on purchase price alone misses the filter schedule, and comparing on months alone misses the gallon ratings that cap each filter in a heavy-use household.
          </p>

          <p>
            AquaTru publishes the clearest schedule: the pre/carbon filter every 6 months or 600 gallons, the VOC filter every 12 months or 600 gallons, and the RO membrane every 24 months or 1,200 gallons. The Carafe's one-year filter combo is listed at $99.85. At a gallon a day that year is 365 gallons, about 27 cents a gallon in filters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Waterdrop's C2SL uses one C2RFL filter, rated for 12 months or 528 gallons and listed at $55.99. At a gallon a day you would replace it on the 12 month limit, about 15 cents a gallon. Bluevua's 5-in-1 filter is listed at $79.90 for about a year, roughly 22 cents a gallon at the same use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add electricity, which is small for a pump that runs a few minutes per batch, and the waste water, which is a third of a gallon per gallon made at 3:1. Over two years, filter cost is usually less than a third of the purchase price on these units. If you are weighing one against an under-sink RO, the under-sink system usually costs less per gallon at high use and more to install.
          </p>

