            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Gravity Pitcher Labeled RO</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pitcher with no plug cannot push water through a reverse osmosis membrane, because a few inches of water make a fraction of a psi. If a gravity pitcher claims RO-level results, it is using ion-exchange resin or carbon and should not carry NSF/ANSI 58. Buy a plug-in countertop unit if you actually need reverse osmosis.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating NSF/ANSI 372 as a Performance Claim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                372 certifies that the materials are low-lead. It says nothing about removing contaminants. A countertop unit certified only to 372 can truthfully say NSF certified. For RO, look for NSF/ANSI 58 with a listed contaminant table, and look the exact model up in the IAPMO, NSF or WQA database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Water Hardness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hard water scales RO membranes. AquaTru's data sheet says not to use its units above 10 grains per gallon hardness without softening first, because scale will plug the membrane. If your water is very hard, check the limit for your unit, expect shorter membrane life, or soften before filtering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading TDS as a Safety Score</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                TDS measures all dissolved solids, mostly harmless minerals. A low reading does not prove lead is gone, and a high one does not mean the water is unsafe. Use the TDS display to track whether the membrane is working over time, and use a certification table or lab test for specific contaminants.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Waste Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Countertop RO units send rejected water to a waste compartment or back to the tap tank, and you empty it. At 3:1, that is a third of a gallon for every gallon you drink. Plan where it goes. Plants that tolerate salt, mopping and rinsing are fine. Drinking it is not.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

