        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a reverse osmosis water filter pitcher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the gravity sense. Reverse osmosis needs pressure that a pitcher cannot supply. What is sold as an RO pitcher is a plug-in countertop unit that pumps tap water through a membrane and delivers it into a carafe or pitcher. The AquaTru Glass Carafe, Waterdrop M6SL and C2SL and Bluevua ROPOT all work that way.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water does a countertop reverse osmosis system waste?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The better units here run about 3:1, three parts purified water to one part waste. Waterdrop lists 3:1 for the M6SL and C2SL, and AquaTru's data sheet gives a 75% efficiency rating, which is the same thing. You pour the waste out by hand, roughly a third of a gallon for every gallon you drink.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does countertop reverse osmosis remove fluoride?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A certified one does. AquaTru's performance data sheet lists 93.5% average fluoride reduction for its Classic family on the NSF/ANSI 58 test. Other makers claim fluoride reduction without publishing a certified figure. Carbon pitchers generally do not remove fluoride, which is one of the main reasons to choose RO over a pitcher.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a remineralization filter on an RO system?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, it is a taste choice. RO removes the minerals that give tap water its flavor, and some people find the result flat. Mineral stages add a small amount back. The amounts are small next to what you get from food, so treat it as flavor, not nutrition. Optional stages let you try both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is countertop RO better than under-sink RO?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It does the same filtering. Countertop RO needs no plumbing and moves with you, which suits renters and full cabinets. Under-sink RO makes water continuously at its own faucet, handles higher daily use and sends waste to the drain. If you own the home and drink a lot of filtered water, under-sink is usually the better long-term buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does NSF/ANSI 58 mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is the drinking water reverse osmosis standard. Certification to 58 means the system's TDS reduction was tested against a public protocol, and lets the maker list specific contaminants such as lead, fluoride, arsenic and nitrate. It is the certification to look for on any RO claim. NSF/ANSI 372, by contrast, only covers lead content of the materials.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you change countertop RO filters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the unit. AquaTru lists its pre/carbon filter at 6 months or 600 gallons, its VOC filter at 12 months or 600 gallons and its RO membrane at 24 months or 1,200 gallons. Waterdrop rates the C2SL filter for 12 months or 528 gallons, and Bluevua says its 5-in-1 filter lasts about a year.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
